If the X half-step atom at depth n and bit-source m is not junk, then the basic neighbourhood of its second-system index is contained in the basic neighbourhood of the atom-pair's second index.

The junk formula and the characterization of a vanishing selection give that the atom-pair is not junk and that the direct intersection is nonempty. The closed form of the half-step index is the positive split index. Code-state correctness, the invariant of the generated pair, and the split specification at the basic neighbourhood of n are available. The intersection of the two first-system pieces is nonempty, so the positive branch of the split is nonempty, and the positive-index specification identifies the half-step neighbourhood with that branch. The branch is contained in the union of the two branches, which the split specification equates with the second-system companion.
